          %p
            The
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadicity+theorem"} monadicity theorem
            characterizes monadic functors and makes these ‘nice properties’ precise.
          %p
            Monadic functors are sometimes called
            = succeed "." do
              %em functors of effective descent (type)
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadic+descent"} monadic descent
            for more on this aspect.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+pair"} pair
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                    %mo :
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon C \to D :U
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \dashv U
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+of+an+adjunction"} unit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
