              If here
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ′
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U' U
              is monadic, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              is of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent+type"} descent type
              and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comparison+functor"} comparison functor
              has a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} left adjoint
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U'
              is furthermore
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conservative"} conservative
              (and in particular if it is monadic), then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              is monadic.
          %p
            This is Propositions 4 and 5 of
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Bourn"} Bourn
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              A monadic functor is
              %em strictly monadic
              if and only if it is also an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/amnestic+functor"} amnestic
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isofibration"} isofibration
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              Clearly, a strictly monadic functor is an amnestic isofibration; and if a monadic functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              is amnestic, then the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comparison+functor"} comparison functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              is also
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/amnestic+functor"} amnestic
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              is a monadic isofibration, so is
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              must be an isomorphism of categories.
          %h3#monadicity_theorem Monadicity theorem
          %p
            Various versions of Beck’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadicity+theorem"} monadicity theorem
            (also: “tripleability theorem” in older literature) give sufficient, and sometimes necessary, conditions for a given functor to be monadic. There are also dual,
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonadic+functor"} comonadic versions
          %h3#monadic_functors_to_set Monadic functors to Set
          %p
            Monadic functors to the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            have additional properties. For example:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Every monadic functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi D
                      %mo →
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \,\colon\, D \to \mathrm{Set}
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+functor"} solid functor
            %li
              %p
                A category is monadic over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Set}
                (i.e. it admits a monadic functor to
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Set}
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+category"} Barr exact
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocomplete+category"} cocomplete
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} projective
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separator"} generator
          %p
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Vitale94"}> Vitale (1994)
            \&amp;rbrack;
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %p
          #ReflectiveSubcategoryInclusion.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              Every
              = succeed "-inclusion" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective subcategory
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadic+functor"} monadic functor
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "reflective+subcategory#ReflectiveSubcategoryInclusionIsMonadic"} there
          %p
            A proof is spelled out for instance in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "reflective+subcategory#Borceux94b"} Borceux 1994, vol 2, cor. 4.2.4
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+proof"} formal proof
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+Agda"} cubical Agda
            is given in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "reflective+subcategory#1Lab"} 1Lab
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "idempotent+monad#AlgebrasForAnIdempotentMonad"} idempotent monad – Properties – Algebras for an idempotent monad and localization
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadic+adjunction"}> monadic adjunction
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure-semantics+adjunction"} structure-semantics adjunction
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonadic+functor"}> comonadic functor
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadicity+theorem"} monadicity theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadic+decomposition"} monadic decomposition
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#MacLane71
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+Mac+Lane"}> Saunders Mac Lane
                , Section VI in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+for+the+Working+Mathematician"} Categories for the Working Mathematician
                %strong 5
                Springer (1971, second ed. 1997) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4757-4721-8"}> doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-4721-8
                \&amp;rbrack;
